# {{ app_name }} — Ethos-U85 TFLite Micro App

NSX app template for running a TFLite Micro model on the Arm Ethos-U85 NPU
({{ board }}). Ships with:

- **nsx-npu** — NPU power-up + Ethos-U core-driver init (`nsx_npu_init`),
  built on top of **nsx-ethos-u-driver** (the board/SoC-agnostic upstream
  Ethos-U driver, pulled in automatically as a `nsx-npu` dependency)
- **nsx-helia-rt** — TFLM runtime with the ethos-u custom-op kernel enabled
  (`NSX_HELIA_RT_ENABLE_ETHOSU=ON`, see `CMakeLists.txt`)
- `src/main.cc` — inference harness (zeros + pseudo-random noise inputs,
  DWT cycle timing, ITM/SWO output)
- `tools/tflite_to_header.py` — model → C++ header converter

## 1. Compile your model with Vela

The NPU executes command streams produced offline by
[Vela](https://pypi.org/project/ethos-u-vela/):

```bash
pip install ethos-u-vela
vela your_model.tflite --accelerator-config ethos-u85-256 --output-dir vela_out
```

`ethos-u85-256` matches the 256-MAC Ethos-U85 configuration on atomiq110.
Vela wraps supported subgraphs in an `ethos-u` custom op; fully-supported
models become a single NPU op (100% offload).

## 2. Embed the model

```bash
python tools/tflite_to_header.py vela_out/your_model_vela.tflite src/model_data.h
```

Until you do this, the app builds with a placeholder and prints these
instructions at runtime instead of running inference.

## 3. Build, flash, view

```bash
nsx build --app-dir .
nsx flash --app-dir .
nsx view  --app-dir .
```

## Memory placement

The tensor arena is placed in shared SRAM via the `.sram_bss` section
(`NOLOAD`, zero-initialized by startup): the Ethos-U85 is a bus master and
**cannot access the core-private TCM**. Keep the arena (and anything the NPU
reads/writes) out of TCM. Use the `.shared` section instead when a buffer
needs copy-initialized contents (it is loaded from NVM at startup, so it
costs image size and boot time). Model weights live in NVM, which the NPU
can read directly.

## Hardware notes

- `src/main.cc` requests HP perf mode; `am_hal_pwrctrl_npu_mode_select`
  works on both silicon and the NPU-enabled FPGA image. Set
  `skip_perf_mode = true` only to bypass mode selection on a custom
  pre-silicon target.
- If the target has no working NPU power domain (e.g. an FPGA image without
  the U85), `nsx_npu_init` fails cleanly and the app prints an actionable
  message instead of bus-faulting.
- If Vela leaves some ops on the CPU (partial offload), add the matching
  `resolver.Add*()` kernels in `src/main.cc` and bump `kNumOps`.
